<section class="faq-section">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-12"><h2 class="title">FAQs</h2></div>
            <div class="col-12 col-md-6">
                <div class="faq-box">
                    <div class="title-box">
                        <img src="./asset/image/ooui_next-ltr.svg" alt="" class="icon">
                        <h3 class="faq-title">How long do solar panels typically last?</h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-content">
                        <p>Solar panels typically last 25 to 30 years or more. Over this time, they gradually lose efficiency, but most panels still operate at about 80% of their original capacity after 25 years.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-box">
                    <div class="title-box">
                        <img src="./asset/image/ooui_next-ltr.svg" alt="" class="icon">
                        <h3 class="faq-title">Do solar panels work on cloudy days or at night?</h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-content">
                        <p>Solar panels generate electricity whenever there’s sunlight, even on cloudy days, although at reduced efficiency. However, they don’t produce electricity at night, so most systems rely on battery storage or grid power after dark.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-box">
                    <div class="title-box">
                        <img src="./asset/image/ooui_next-ltr.svg" alt="" class="icon">
                        <h3 class="faq-title">How much maintenance do solar panels require?</h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-content">
                        <p>Solar panels require minimal maintenance. Occasional cleaning to remove dust or debris is usually enough, and routine inspections every few years can help ensure optimal performance.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-12 col-md-6">
                <div class="faq-box">
                    <div class="title-box">
                        <img src="./asset/image/ooui_next-ltr.svg" alt="" class="icon">
                        <h3 class="faq-title">Can solar panels withstand extreme weather conditions?</h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-content">
                        <p>Yes, high-quality solar panels are designed to withstand various weather conditions, including snow, hail, and strong winds. They’re rigorously tested to ensure durability, but it’s always good to check local guidelines and installation standards.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-box">
                    <div class="title-box">
                        <img src="./asset/image/ooui_next-ltr.svg" alt="" class="icon">
                        <h3 class="faq-title">How much can I save on my electric bill by switching to solar?</h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-content">
                        <p>Savings depend on factors like energy usage, local rates, and the size of your solar system. On average, homeowners can save $100 to $300 a month, with some even eliminating their electric bills entirely.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="faq-box">
                    <div class="title-box">
                        <img src="./asset/image/ooui_next-ltr.svg" alt="" class="icon">
                        <h3 class="faq-title">Are there any government incentives for installing solar panels?</h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-content">
                        <p>Yes, many countries and states offer incentives, tax credits, and rebates to reduce the cost of installing solar panels. It’s a good idea to check with local agencies for available programs in your area.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>